bly everywhere, at similar magnitudes, regardless of culture—like strength does. Instead we see: - Countries with more gender equality having smaller or no gaps - The gap shifting over decades within the same countries as opportunities for women changed - Girls outperforming boys in math in some countries entirely That pattern points strongly to environment and opportunity, not biology. I know this is a really common assumption to pick up, since it's repeated a lot and the strength comparison feels intuitive. But when people have actually gone looking for the "boys' brains are built better for math" mechanism, they haven't found it—they've found effects of stereotyping and unequal encouragement instead.
