13:29:53 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Actually, worth correcting the premise here: people *did* notice, for a very long time — they just weren't in a position to be heard or believed. **Women have been pushing back on this specific belief for well over a century** Women fighting for access to universities in the 1800s, arguing they were just as capable of rigorous study, weren't a 2020s invention — that was a live, explicit argument in the 1800s and early 1900s, against fierce resistance. Women who did excel in math and science (and there were some, even when barred from most institutions) were routinely dismissed as "exceptions" rather than treated as counter-evidence. So the objection you're raising now — "wait, is this actually true?" — isn't new. It's an old argument that kept losing, not because it was weak, but because the people making it had little institutional power. **"Nobody noticed until recent studies" undersells how gradual and contested this was** This wasn't "ancient unanimous belief, then suddenly overturned by a few papers." It was: - A belief enforced partly by *excluding women from the chance to disprove it* (barred from universities, professional societies, publishing under their own names) - Slowly eroded over more than a century as those barriers were removed one by one - Tested more rigorously as research methods, statistics, and international data collection matured in the 20th century - Confirmed by *increasingly large, cross-cultural datasets*, not a sudden ideological reversal So it's less "everyone agreed for millennia, then suddenly modern researchers said otherwise," and more "a belief that benefited existing power structures was enforced through actual exclusion, contested the whole time by the people it excluded, and gradually lost ground as that exclusion lifted and better data came in." **A useful comparison** Similar things happened with race and intelligence, land ownership rights, and plenty of other domains — the "obvious truth" was obvious mainly to the group it favored, while the group it disadvantaged was saying "wait, no" the entire time, just without the power to be taken seriously. That pattern showing up again here isn't a coincidence — it's what happens whenever there's an unequal starting position between who holds a belief and who bears its cost. You're not wrong that it seems strange for something to be "wrong for that long." I'd just push back on "nobody noticed" — plenty of people noticed. They just weren't the ones writing the textbooks.
